mmatical sentence. The nonfinite participle &ldquo;having explained&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite participle &ldquo;explaining&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The nonfinite to-infinitive &ldquo;to explain&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t supply the main clause with a finite verb. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-741-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e15aaa3c-a31b-4c06-9d3d-9e77663ee990","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$5aa171de$SATQ$, $SATQ$e1514a4c-0079-4703-a68b-9704e381a0c9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Chondrites are stony meteorites that are undifferentiated&mdash;that is, their contents have not melted and separated into distinct layers. They are hardly <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> many chondrites experience aqueous alteration as a result of exposure to fluids, as well as fracturing, veining, and localized melting due to collisions with other objects. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4b1fa5be-e74b-4ead-b0fe-4163869b1986","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though</p>"},{"id":"14217a86-f300-4d37-b883-f9236375805e","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"},{"id":"2e86eb82-dfc2-43ca-91fa-bd705430955d","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> though&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"372816fe-c77e-4d6d-ab03-019b2313a87c","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["14217a86-f300-4d37-b883-f9236375805e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb "though" from the preceding main clause ("They are hardly pristine") and uses a semicolon to join the two main clauses ("They&hellip;though" and "many&hellip;objects"). Further, placing the semicolon after "though" indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (chondrites are far from pristine) is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (chondrites have been generally unaltered by their environment).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because placing the comma after "pristine" and using "though" as a subordinating conjunction illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (many chondrites have experienced damage) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (chondrites are far from pristine). Choice C is incorrect because placing the semicolon after "pristine" illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (many chondrites have experienced damage) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (chondrites are far from pristine). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, the comma after "though" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5aa171de","external_id":"e1514a4c-0079-4703-a68b-9704e381a0c9","vaultid":null,"uId":"80007929-13fc-4ff8-a4ed-c985dea0dbb7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Chondrites are stony meteorites that are undifferentiated&mdash;that is, their contents have not melted and separated into distinct layers. They are hardly <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> many chondrites experience aqueous alteration as a result of exposure to fluids, as well as fracturing, veining, and localized melting due to collisions with other objects. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4b1fa5be-e74b-4ead-b0fe-4163869b1986","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though</p>"},{"id":"14217a86-f300-4d37-b883-f9236375805e","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span></p>"},{"id":"2e86eb82-dfc2-43ca-91fa-bd705430955d","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> though&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"372816fe-c77e-4d6d-ab03-019b2313a87c","content":"<p>pristine<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}],"keys":["14217a86-f300-4d37-b883-f9236375805e"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb \"though\" from the preceding main clause (\"They are hardly pristine\") and uses a semicolon to join the two main clauses (\"They&hellip;though\" and \"many&hellip;objects\"). Further, placing the semicolon after \"though\" indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (chondrites are far from pristine) is contrary to what might be assumed from the information in the previous sentence (chondrites have been generally unaltered by their environment).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because placing the comma after \"pristine\" and using \"though\" as a subordinating conjunction illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (many chondrites have experienced damage) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (chondrites are far from pristine). Choice C is incorrect because placing the semicolon after \"pristine\" illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (many chondrites have experienced damage) is contrary to the information in the previous clause (chondrites are far from pristine). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, the comma after \"though\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669214,"updateDate":1743430669214}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669214, 1743430669214),
    ($SATQ$5aa1fffd$SATQ$, $SATQ$97a19958-6b28-4e97-925a-daeba4ca7826$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Where <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Interestingly, it was invented by an author. It first appears in the novel <em>Through the Looking Glass</em> by English author Lewis Carroll.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f0da1d89-d096-4722-b342-a0317632e271","content":"<p>did the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"be9bfe06-0ea0-4e9a-be3c-c0a39315f146","content":"<p>the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; did come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"1a0b2c36-081f-4a54-82a0-919f5681dd94","content":"<p>did the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"e07f39e8-7b6a-42f2-8e55-4e7504a079eb","content":"<p>the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1a0b2c36-081f-4a54-82a0-919f5681dd94"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a question mark to punctuate the interrogative sentence "where did the word &lsquo;chortle&rsquo; come from," which asks a direct question.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a period can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate an interrogative sentence, such as "where did the word &lsquo;chortle&rsquo; come from." Choice B is incorrect because "where the word &lsquo;chortle&rsquo; did come from" does not follow the conventional structure of a direct question in Standard English. It&rsquo;s conventional in Standard English to follow the interrogative word "where" with an auxiliary verb (such as "did") when asking a direct question. Choice D is incorrect because "where the word &lsquo;chortle&rsquo; come from" does not follow the conventional structure of a direct question in Standard English. It&rsquo;s conventional in Standard English to follow the interrogative word "where" with an auxiliary verb (such as "did") when asking a direct question. Furthermore, a period can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate an interrogative sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5aa1fffd","external_id":"97a19958-6b28-4e97-925a-daeba4ca7826","vaultid":null,"uId":"cb0f1002-4286-4104-9dd4-de2996a8f3a7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Where <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Interestingly, it was invented by an author. It first appears in the novel <em>Through the Looking Glass</em> by English author Lewis Carroll.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f0da1d89-d096-4722-b342-a0317632e271","content":"<p>did the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"be9bfe06-0ea0-4e9a-be3c-c0a39315f146","content":"<p>the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; did come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"1a0b2c36-081f-4a54-82a0-919f5681dd94","content":"<p>did the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"e07f39e8-7b6a-42f2-8e55-4e7504a079eb","content":"<p>the word &ldquo;chortle&rdquo; come from<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"}]